from assemble_topic import assemble, W, read, dump, stamp
FACTS={
'01':('Renewable energy sources are replenished by natural processes on a human timescale, while finite non-renewable fuel stocks are not replaced at a useful consumption rate.','Electricity is an energy carrier whose source classification depends on generation, not a single primary source.'),
'01-01':('Sunlight, wind and flowing water provide renewable energy flows with location and timing constraints.','Renewability does not establish unlimited usable output or an absence of environmental effects.'),
'01-02':('Coal, oil and natural gas are finite fossil fuels formed through long geological processes.','Uranium is non-renewable but is not a fossil fuel.'),
'02':('Fossil supply depends on extraction, infrastructure, delivery and conversion as well as deposits.','Fuel availability and electricity delivered are different stages of an energy supply chain.'),
'02-01':('Coal combustion can produce heat for a steam-turbine generating process.','Coal use involves fuel supply, emissions and ash-management requirements.'),
'02-02':('Oil is refined into products including petrol and diesel for transport and other energy uses.','Imported petroleum costs can be affected by world prices, exchange rates and supply-chain conditions.'),
'02-03':('Natural gas is a fossil fuel largely composed of methane and requires suitable transport and conversion systems.','Gas combustion emits carbon dioxide and upstream methane leakage can add climate effects.'),
'02-04':('Wood biomass can be replenished where suitable regrowth keeps pace with harvesting.','Harvesting faster than regrowth depletes local woodland despite the possibility of plant recovery.'),
'03':('Renewables can displace some fossil use where delivered output matches the service and system conditions.','Changes in energy shares and absolute amounts are different measures of dependence.'),
'03-01':('Geothermal energy uses heat from within the Earth rather than a hot surface climate.','Usable geothermal supply depends on suitable geology, access and resource management.'),
'03-02':('Wave energy converts motion of surface waves using suitable devices.','A wave project depends on wave conditions, equipment survival, access and delivery infrastructure.'),
'03-03':('Tidal energy uses tidal range or currents with predictable but varying timing.','Tidal suitability depends on local range or flow and relevant ecological or use constraints.'),
'03-04':('Hydroelectric generation uses water flow through usable head to turn turbines and generators.','A renewable water cycle does not prevent low-flow limits at a particular station.'),
'03-05':('Photovoltaic cells convert light directly into electricity, while solar thermal systems capture heat.','Solar generation changes with sunlight and a night service needs suitable storage or another supply.'),
'03-06':('Biogas is produced through managed oxygen-free decomposition of suitable organic feedstock.','A sustained biogas service needs feedstock, appropriate operating conditions, handling and maintenance.'),
'04':('HEP siting needs suitable usable flow, head, physical works and delivery connections.','NamPower identifies Ruacana on the Kunene as run-of-the-river hydropower rather than unlimited seasonal storage.'),
'05':('Water directed through a turbine drives rotation and a connected generator produces electricity.','Water continues downstream through an outlet or tailrace while electricity follows a separate transmission path.'),
'06':('Energy options should be compared against a stated service, time, cost and environmental criterion.','Maximum power capacity and delivered energy over time do not establish the same thing.'),
'06-01':('HEP avoids fuel combustion during generation but can alter river or land conditions.','Run-of-river output remains constrained by flow and cannot be credited with unprovided storage.'),
'06-02':('Coal can support controllable generation where fuel and equipment are available.','Combustion and the wider coal chain create emissions, waste and continuing costs.'),
'06-03':('Nuclear fission can supply low-direct-carbon generating heat while requiring finite fuel and complex safety systems.','Nuclear investment, radioactive waste and decommissioning requirements remain relevant to comparison.'),
'06-04':('Solar PV avoids on-site combustion but its direct output depends on sunlight.','Storage involves capacity, power limits, losses and cost rather than creating additional primary energy.'),
'07':('Wind and solar siting combines measured resource, access, foundations or structure, delivery and environmental conditions.','A strong resource alone does not establish a feasible dependable service.'),
'07-01':('Wind assessment requires suitable measurements over time at relevant height, with attention to turbulence and extremes.','Grid capacity, construction access and affected habitats or people can constrain a wind site.'),
'07-02':('Solar siting depends on radiation, shading, layout, suitable space and delivery arrangements.','High air temperature alone does not establish the strongest useful photovoltaic output.'),
'08':('Energy-project assessment examines relevant impacts, alternatives and management before decisions and later monitoring.','Policy activity counts are distinct from observed environmental, resource or household outcomes.'),
'08-01':('An assessment should follow the actual project’s land, water, emissions and community pathways.','A mitigation plan needs implementation and outcome evidence rather than a title alone.'),
'08-02':('Woodland recovery must be compared with harvesting and other losses over the same interval.','Planting counts are not equivalent to surviving established replacement stock.'),
'08-03':('Open fires and inefficient household combustion can produce harmful air pollution.','Cleaner usable services require suitable technology, affordability, reliability and appropriate safety arrangements.'),
'09':('Useful energy access supports services and enterprises through the required rate and timing of supply.','Namibia’s 2022 integrated resource planning concerns electricity investment pathways, not guaranteed current outcomes.'),
'10':('Namibia’s electricity links to the southern African region create exchange opportunities subject to supply, contracts and network capacity.','Petroleum supply connects Namibia to global production, refining, transport and pricing.')}
CARDS={
'001':('Distinguish renewable from non-renewable energy, using sunlight and coal and separating replenishment from recycling.','Renewable sunlight is naturally supplied on a human timescale, whereas coal is a finite fossil stock not replaced at its consumption rate. Recycling equipment does not replenish coal or define the sunlight flow.',['1 mark for the renewable side of the difference; 1 for the finite-stock contrast; 1 for the replenishment/recycling distinction.']),
'002':('Suggest how to classify fuelwood where regrowth is twenty units and harvest thirty over the same interval, other changes absent. Explain the stock implication.','Wood biomass can be renewable through regrowth, but this harvesting pattern depletes the local stock by ten units. Sustainable replenishment needs recovery to match use rather than the mere possibility of growth.',['1 mark for the classification in the given context; 1 for the regrowth/harvest reason; 1 for applying the ten-unit depletion condition.']),
'003':('Name one renewable flow usable for electricity generation.','Sunlight, wind or suitable flowing water.',['1 mark for a relevant source name.']),
'004':('Name a non-renewable fuel that is not fossil.','Uranium.',['1 mark for the fuel name uranium.']),
'005':('Explain how a country can use a fossil fuel despite having no domestic extraction of it.','Extraction elsewhere can supply trade. Suitable transport, storage and purchasing arrangements deliver imported fuel to local conversion equipment. It can then provide a service, while continued availability depends on that supply chain.',['1 mark for external extraction as a factor; 1 for the trade/delivery link; 1 for suitable conversion; 1 for its conditional local-service effect.']),
'006':('Describe the supply-chain record: a station has a working generator but no fuel after a shipment delay; the overseas deposit is unchanged.','The immediate fuel-delivery stage is constrained despite functioning conversion equipment. The deposit record does not resolve the local stock shortage. The evidence concerns access and delivery, not disappearance of the geological resource.',['1 mark for the interrupted-chain pattern; 1 for working-generator/deposit evidence; 1 for the availability distinction.']),
'007':('Name the solid fossil fuel commonly burned for heat in a steam-generating power process.','Coal.',['1 mark for the fuel name coal.']),
'008':('Name the liquid fossil resource refined into petrol and diesel.','Oil or petroleum.',['1 mark for the resource name oil/petroleum.']),
'009':('Name the fossil fuel largely composed of methane.','Natural gas.',['1 mark for the fuel name natural gas.']),
'010':('Name the condition needed for harvested wood biomass to maintain its local stock, with other losses accounted for.','Suitable regrowth or replenishment must keep pace with harvesting and other losses.',['1 mark for the replenishment/harvest-balance condition point.']),
'011':('Explain how suitable renewable generation can reduce imported fossil use where its output matches the required service.','A usable local renewable flow supplies generation. When that delivered output replaces an otherwise fossil-supplied load, less fossil fuel is needed for that service. The reduction depends on actual displacement and timing rather than installation alone.',['1 mark for local resource as a factor; 1 for the generation-to-load link; 1 for displaced fossil operation; 1 for the conditional import/fuel effect.']),
'012':('Describe the supplied three-month chart: solar output 2,4,6 energy units and hydro output 6,4,2 in the same months. Demand is not shown.','Solar rises while hydro falls. Their combined monthly output remains eight units. The chart supports offsetting component trends but does not establish demand coverage or the rest of the year.',['1 mark for the opposite-series pattern; 1 for the constant-total chart evidence; 1 for the demand/time limitation.']),
'013':('Name the source using heat from within the Earth rather than incoming sunlight.','Geothermal energy.',['1 mark for the source name geothermal.']),
'014':('Name the renewable source using motion of surface sea waves.','Wave energy.',['1 mark for the source name wave energy.']),
'015':('Name the source using tidal water-level differences or tidal currents.','Tidal energy.',['1 mark for the source name tidal energy.']),
'016':('Name the electricity-generation source using moving water through a usable height difference.','Hydroelectric power.',['1 mark for the source name hydroelectric power/HEP.']),
'017':('Name the solar technology converting light directly into electricity.','Photovoltaic cells or solar PV.',['1 mark for the technology name photovoltaic/PV.']),
'018':('Name the fuel produced through suitable organic material decomposing without oxygen in a managed digester.','Biogas.',['1 mark for the fuel name biogas.']),
'019':('Explain why suitable flow and head together matter when locating hydroelectric generation.','Flow provides water moving through the system and head provides a usable height difference. Their combination supplies energy to the turbine, which drives generation. A high fall with inadequate flow can still limit useful output.',['1 mark for flow as a factor; 1 for its head/energy link; 1 for turbine-driven generation; 1 for the conditional output effect.']),
'020':('Describe the site evidence: A has substantial head but unreliable low flow; B has suitable head and a more dependable usable flow, with other supplied conditions comparable.','A’s height advantage is constrained by water availability. B has the stated flow-reliability advantage alongside usable head. The record favours B on those resource conditions but does not supply complete costs or environmental assessment.',['1 mark for the paired-resource pattern; 1 for flow/head evidence; 1 for the bounded location interpretation.']),
'021':('Describe the hydroelectric generation sequence from directed water to electrical supply.','Water moves through a usable head and turns a turbine; the connected generator converts rotation into electricity; electrical equipment and connections deliver supply while water continues downstream.',['1 mark for the first water/turbine step; 1 for generator conversion; 1 for the separated water and electrical-output result.']),
'022':('Describe the process contrast: arrow A carries water through a turbine to a tailrace; arrow B connects the generator to transmission.','A is the continuing water-flow path and B the electrical-delivery path. Turbine rotation links water energy to the generator. The water is not burned or converted wholesale into electricity.',['1 mark for the two-path relationship; 1 for turbine/generator evidence; 1 for the conversion distinction.']),
'023':('Explain why a solar-only installation without storage or another connection cannot by itself meet a load after sunset merely because it generated enough earlier.','Solar output depends on sunlight, which is absent during the stated load. Earlier energy needs suitable storage to be shifted to that time. Without storage or another supply, the timing gap remains despite a sufficient daytime total.',['1 mark for sunlight timing as a factor; 1 for the generation/load link; 1 for the storage requirement; 1 for the unmet-service effect under the stated conditions.']),
'024':('Describe the comparison: HEP has variable usable river flow; coal needs recurring fuel deliveries; nuclear needs fuel and radioactive-waste arrangements; solar PV has no direct night generation.','The technologies have different resource and operational constraints. Water variability, delivered fuel, waste responsibilities and sunlight timing affect different parts of supply. The evidence does not rank total cost or establish one source as best for every service.',['1 mark for the differentiated-constraint pattern; 1 for at least two correct paired evidence examples; 1 for the limited ranking interpretation.']),
'025':('How far do you agree that Ruacana’s renewable source proves constant output in every month? NamPower identifies it as run-of-the-river hydropower, and usable flow varies. Judge dependable generation.','Disagree with constant output being established. Renewable river supply does not mean constant usable flow. Run-of-river generation remains constrained by that flow and operating conditions; a maximum rating or the source label cannot replace actual generation evidence.',['Up to 4 C marks for judgement using the case’s design evidence, flow dependence and rating/output distinction.','1 C mark for a supported dependable-generation decision.']),
'026':('How far do you agree that a coal generator’s presence alone establishes dependable supply? Its equipment works but no fuel is available and no delivery is arranged.','Disagree. Conversion capacity exists, but the required fuel input is absent. A usable continuing fuel chain is needed alongside equipment and connections. Dependability is not established by ownership or maximum capacity alone.',['Up to 4 C marks for judgement using the functioning-equipment evidence, absent input and supply-chain conditions.','1 C mark for a supported decision against established supply.']),
'027':('How far do you agree that low direct operating carbon emissions make nuclear power renewable and free of waste responsibilities? It uses finite uranium and produces radioactive materials requiring management.','Disagree. Low direct carbon emissions describe one operating characteristic, not fuel replenishment. Finite uranium remains non-renewable, while radioactive-material and decommissioning responsibilities remain. The emissions benefit is real without establishing those separate claims.',['Up to 4 C marks for judgement using the emissions/replenishment distinction and supplied waste evidence.','1 C mark for a supported decision rejecting the combined claim.']),
'028':('How far do you agree that a solar store charged with ten energy units can supply a ten-unit evening load? Under the stated losses only eight units can be delivered, and no other supply is available.','Disagree: deliverable energy is eight units, leaving a two-unit gap. Storage shifts energy with losses; it does not create the missing amount. Adequacy also depends on timing and discharge power, which would need to match the load.',['Up to 4 C marks for judgement using input/delivered evidence, the two-unit deficit and relevant storage conditions.','1 C mark for a supported decision against full-load coverage.']),
'029':('Explain why strong measured wind alone does not establish the best location for delivered electricity.','The wind resource offers generating potential, but construction access and a usable connection are also required. If delivery is constrained, some potential cannot become the intended service. Cost and environmental conditions can therefore alter a resource-only ranking.',['1 mark for wind resource as a factor; 1 for the access/connection link; 1 for constrained delivery; 1 for its location-choice effect.']),
'030':('Describe the siting contrast: A has strong measured wind but no feasible grid route; B has a somewhat weaker resource and a usable connection. No complete costs are supplied.','A leads on the stated resource measure, while B leads on connection feasibility. The resource and delivered-service rankings can differ. The record does not establish an exact cost advantage or final overall winner.',['1 mark for the resource/connection pattern; 1 for paired site evidence; 1 for the bounded ranking interpretation.']),
'031':('Name one wind measurement requirement stronger than a single ground-level gust observation.','A suitable time series at relevant turbine height.',['1 mark for a relevant measurement requirement point.']),
'032':('Name one solar-location condition, besides radiation, affecting useful output.','Limited shading, suitable orientation/space, structural support or another appropriate condition.',['1 mark for a relevant condition name.']),
'033':('Explain how harvesting wood faster than regrowth can affect both the resource and household access.','Removal exceeds replacement, reducing local stock. Less nearby wood can lengthen collection journeys and increase labour needs. Continued depletion can undermine later fuel access unless use and suitable recovery are brought into balance.',['1 mark for excess removal as a factor; 1 for the stock-decline link; 1 for the collection/access effect; 1 for its continued household-resource consequence.']),
'034':('Describe the woodland record: 1,000 seedlings were planted, 300 survive after a dry period and no later growth record is supplied.','Planting input and established survival differ. The record supports 300 survivors, not 1,000 replacement trees. It does not yet establish that growth matches ongoing harvesting.',['1 mark for the input/survival pattern; 1 for numerical evidence; 1 for the replenishment limitation.']),
'035':('Name the process examining likely project effects, alternatives and management before relevant development decisions.','Environmental impact assessment.',['1 mark for the process name environmental impact assessment/EIA.']),
'036':('Name the resource condition where fuelwood harvesting and other losses exceed woodland recovery.','Woodland depletion or declining wood stock.',['1 mark for the condition name woodland depletion.']),
'037':('Name one household energy health pressure from open fires or inefficient combustion.','Harmful household air pollution or smoke exposure.',['1 mark for a relevant health-pressure name.']),
'038':('Explain how dependable usable energy can support a small food enterprise where refrigeration is needed.','Suitable power and energy allow refrigeration to operate at required times. This can reduce temperature-related spoilage under appropriate handling, retaining more saleable food. The benefit depends on affordability, functioning equipment and the service actually supplied.',['1 mark for dependable supply as a factor; 1 for the refrigeration link; 1 for reduced spoilage under suitable conditions; 1 for the usable-output effect.']),
'039':('Describe the access record: a household has a small lighting connection, but its capacity and budget do not support the stated cooking appliance.','Lighting access exists while the cooking service remains constrained. The evidence concerns different service levels. A connection count alone does not establish usable access for every household energy need.',['1 mark for the differentiated-service pattern; 1 for capacity/budget evidence; 1 for the access-measure limitation.']),
'040':('Explain how a global oil-price increase can affect a country relying on imported petroleum products.','Higher world supply costs can raise import costs, with the local effect also depending on exchange rates and domestic arrangements. Delivered fuel can then become more costly for transport or machinery, affecting users and supply chains. An exact retail change needs current local evidence.',['1 mark for world price as a factor; 1 for the import-cost link; 1 for delivered-use costs; 1 for the conditional wider economic effect.']),
'041':('Describe what a regional electricity map establishes when it shows a transmission connection but no available-capacity or contract figures.','It shows a route for potential exchange. It does not show the quantity available at each time or unlimited import capability. Supply, agreements and network conditions are needed to assess dependable delivery.',['1 mark for the connection pattern; 1 for the map’s present/missing evidence; 1 for the bounded exchange interpretation.']),
'042':('Explain the error in calling coal renewable because its stove can be reused. What test discriminates source renewability?','The confusion substitutes equipment reuse for source replenishment. Coal’s finite geological stock is not replaced at its use rate. The test asks how the energy source is replenished on human timescales, not whether the appliance survives another use.',['1 mark for the reuse/replenishment confusion; 1 for the correct source distinction and discriminating timescale test.'])}
TASKS=[
{'objectives':['01','03','05'],'prompt':'(a) Name the solar technology converting light directly to electricity. [1]\n(b) Describe three linked steps in hydroelectric generation. [3]\n(c) Name one non-renewable fossil fuel. [1]\n(d) Describe the component and combined-output pattern: solar 2,4,6 units; hydro 6,4,2 units across the same three months. [2]','answer':'(a) Photovoltaic/PV. (b) Directed water through head turns a turbine; the connected generator converts rotation to electricity; suitable electrical connections deliver it while water exits downstream. (c) Coal, oil or natural gas. (d) Solar rises as hydro falls; combined output stays eight units per month.','guide':['(a) 1 A mark for PV.','(b) 3 A marks for three coherent process point descriptions.','(c) 1 A mark for a relevant fossil-fuel name.','(d) 2 B marks for the opposing components and constant combined total.'],'split':{'A':5,'B':2},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Describe',3,'A'),('c','Name',1,'A'),('d','Describe',2,'B')]},
{'objectives':['01','03','06'],'prompt':'A hypothetical three-month record gives solar output 2,4,6 energy units and hydro output 6,4,2 units. Demand and the other nine months are not supplied.\n(a) Name the renewable source represented by hydro output. [1]\n(b) Calculate the three-month combined generation total. [2]\n(c) Describe three distinct features of the paired series. [3]\n(d) What judgement is supported about the claim “the record proves annual demand is fully met”? [1]','answer':'(a) Running water/hydroelectric power. (b) Monthly totals are 8,8,8, summing to 24 energy units. (c) Solar rises by four units; hydro falls by four; combined output is constant at eight per month. (d) Reject the adequacy claim: annual generation and demand are missing. The supplied series alone cannot show coverage.','guide':['(a) 1 A mark for the source name.','(b) 2 B marks for adding both series over all three months and the correct total with stated energy unit.','(c) 3 B marks for the two different component trends and the combined pattern, using resource values.','(d) 1 C mark for the supported judgement based on absent demand/time evidence.'],'split':{'A':1,'B':5,'C':1},'parts':[('a','Name',1,'A'),('b','Calculate',2,'B'),('c','Describe',3,'B'),('d','What',1,'C')]},
{'objectives':['04','06','10'],'prompt':'How far do you agree that Ruacana’s existence proves Namibia needs no other electricity source or regional exchange? NamPower identifies Ruacana as run-of-the-river hydropower. River flow and electricity demand vary, and no complete generation/demand time series or storage provision is supplied here. Judge adequacy and diversity of supply. [5]','answer':'Disagree that the conclusion is established. Ruacana provides a renewable contribution, but run-of-river output depends on usable flow and cannot be credited with unprovided seasonal storage. Demand varies too, and the missing matched time series prevents an adequacy conclusion. Other domestic sources, suitable storage or regional exchange can contribute under their own conditions. Their necessity or precise quantity must be assessed from actual supply and demand rather than the existence of one station.','guide':['Up to 4 C marks for judgement using the case design, flow dependence, demand/time evidence gap and conditional system alternatives.','1 C mark for a supported decision rejecting the unsupported self-sufficiency inference.'],'split':{'C':5},'parts':[('a','How far do you agree',5,'C')]}
]
SOURCES=[('EIA-SOURCES','https://www.eia.gov/energyexplained/what-is-energy/sources-of-energy.php'),('EIA-RENEWABLES','https://www.eia.gov/energyexplained/renewable-sources/types-and-usage.php'),('NAMPOWER-GENERATION','https://www.nampower.com.na/About-Us/'),('MME-NIRP-2022','https://www.mme.gov.na/files/publications/611_NIRP_2022for_theElectricitySupplyIndustry_NamibiaSigned.pdf'),('MME-PLANNING','https://www.mme.gov.na/energy-planning-and-research/'),('MEFT-ENVIRONMENTAL-ASSESSMENT','https://meft.gov.na/services/environmental-impact-assessment-/233/'),('WHO-HOUSEHOLD-AIR','https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/household-air-pollution-and-health'),('WHO-CLEAN-ENERGY','https://www.who.int/teams/environment-climate-change-and-health/air-quality-energy-and-health/sectoral-interventions/household-air-pollution/strategies')]
